{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_avlm75y5mphyp4isvs4pmkolvileitvrbi4a44t3hvohayi2rewq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases passed, and the process found and fixed **two real divergences**.\n\n## What the library is\n\nOne C translation unit (`c_src/src/lib.c`, 191 lines) exporting 10 functions: four arithmetic kernels, a function-pointer dispatcher (`get_operation`), two driver functions that log to `stdout` via `printf`, a checksum, state init/apply, and the `checkshift` one-shot wrapper.\n\n## Phase results\n\n| Phase | Result |\n|---|---|\n| **A** — `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (16 rows + 7 generic), `CONFIGS.md` (31 rows + 5 hardening) | written, mechanically derived from the C |\n| **B** — every `CONFIGS.md` row, randomized, fixed seed | 39 tests pass |\n| **C** — every `ERRORS.md` row | 22 tests pass |\n| **D** — symbol parity + all configurations | diff empty both ways; 69 tests × 2 profiles |\n\nSymbol parity was exact from the start (10/10, confirmed with both `nm -D` and `readelf --dyn-syms`) — which is precisely why the gate isn't \"symbols match.\" Both bugs lived behind matching symbols and a byte-identical happy path.\n\n## Bug 1 — `checkshift`'s allocation-failure branch was optimized away\n\nLLVM recognized the `malloc`/`free` pair, saw the pointer never escaped, and deleted the allocation. The release `.so` contained **no `malloc` call at all** in `checkshift`, so C's `return -1` path was reachable while Rust's was dead code.\n\nSince no argument can trigger it, I reached it with an `LD_PRELOAD` interposer failing exactly 12-byte allocations (armed only after `dlopen`), driving both `.so`s out-of-process. C printed the diagnostic and returned `-1`; Rust ran to completion. A size-logging interposer confirmed it: C asked for `malloc(12)`, Rust asked for nothing. Fixed with `core::hint::black_box` to force the pointer to escape — the `.so` now emits `mov $0xc,%edi; call *…<malloc>` plus the matching `free`.\n\n## Bug 2 — unaligned `ComputeState*` aborted the process\n\n`(*state).field` is an *aligned* access. For a caller-supplied misaligned pointer, gcc's plain x86 load works fine, but Rust's misaligned-pointer check **SIGABRTs** — a crash-versus-works divergence on identical input. Fixed by routing all field access through `read_unaligned`/`write_unaligned` over `addr_of!`.\n\nThis one only appeared under the **debug** profile (release has no alignment check, and x86 tolerates unaligned loads) — a concrete case of why the gate demands every configuration, not just the default.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n- I found `printf` → `puts` rewriting in the Rust output. I verified this is *not* a divergence (`puts` appends the newline; gcc does the same at `-O2`) rather than \"fixing\" it — confirmed by byte-exact transcripts. `FINDINGS.md` records this and four other investigated non-divergences so the distinction is auditable.\n- My first stdout comparison failed spuriously: libtest's own progress output from a parallel thread leaked into the fd-1 capture window. That was my harness's flaw, not the translation's. Fixed with `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` in `.cargo/config.toml` plus a contamination detector that reports the cause instead of a phantom divergence.\n\nThere are no `[features]` and no `#ifdef`s anywhere, so the feature axis collapses to one configuration; `verify.sh` enumerates the powerset generically and covers both profiles. `c_src/` is byte-for-byte unmodified (md5 verified)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":683,"cache_read_input_tokens":178805,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":683,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a8b02a5-cfd1-4565-9dea-c062652ecbe0","uuid":"82cb60de-a3ab-40b4-a1ac-1cbd12677b9a","timestamp":"2026-08-27T23:30:56.216Z"}
